{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: definition succession for a long-lived scope. How an application that is extended more often than replaced moves to a new definition and keeps its record's meaning.\n\nWhy now (Hugh, 22 October 2026). Hugh asked to look at dap as a new operating system: a place where custom-built, often single-purpose applications run for a long time and are retired or extended more often than replaced, while the system itself is still in a research phase. The one lesson that changes the contract is this one, and he asked for it to be done immediately. The persistent systems that lasted are the ones where each new version of a running thing declares how to convert the previous version's state and the platform runs that conversion at an exact point in the order. The persistent systems without that story did not last. In dap an application is a schema plus a policy plus views, and the computation runs in agents and in the shared derivation code, so this is a deterministic schema-migration problem over a replayable record, not a hot code swap.\n\nWhere dap stands. A scope pins one definition at genesis and keeps it for life (docs/scopes.md, docs/what-is-dap.md, docs/architecture.md). A room activates new definitions for new scopes; no scope moves to a new definition. The packages note (notes/2026-10-18-application-packages-and-builder-room.md, section 2) says a template's new version reaches rooms by a successor package, never by propagation, and section 6 item 6 says a successor package names its predecessor and carries forward (M3) without saying how. The roadmap row 'Rules that change after creation' is 'Not designed in dap; tested in the dap-spike'. Earlier notes left the same gap: the jam room note (notes/2026-10-01-jam-room.md, 'How a running room moves to a new definition is not a question this note answers') and the collections note (notes/2026-10-02-collections-of-rooms.md, C-3 'Taking a new definition is a proposal in the cell, landed under the cell's own policy; an agent may make the proposal, it may not skip review', and 'Room succession: a registry binding never moves', R-GEN-13). Acts addressed to another incarnation are already refused, and ReceiveType.from.pin refuses a sender with the declared name but another digest as wrong-pin.\n\nWhat the dap-spike already established (read first; Hugh expects it to carry applicable lessons). The design is ~/play/dap-spike/notes/2026-09-14-evolving-spaces-design.md: section 3 (expected binding and stale_binding: an intent signed against a binding that is no longer active at its position is ineffective, never rewritten; the binding identity is per kind, so an unrelated change does not stale a shared act; a reader that cannot fetch a package pauses, and a pause is never a verdict), section 6 (an attach at position n is judged under the preceding environment, activates from n+1 with its state initialization or migration, bindings and outcome as one atomic transition; an ineffective attach leaves the old environment; no backfill: a model never applies to events before its activation), section 7 (nine versioning rules: old events never change meaning; upgrades activate at an exact ordered boundary; event kinds are immutable contracts and a later model may handle several generations; model migration is explicit and deterministic and pinned at the boundary; large discontinuities may create a new context; presentation versions are independent; grants pin exact capability ids and a newer package never silently enlarges authority; signed attempts preserve their expected binding; replay retains the complete closure), and section 10 invariant 7 (semantic changes are explicit ordered events and never reinterpret history). The results are in ~/play/dap-spike/spike/REPORT.md: a package attached part-way through a live context, earlier outcomes replayed unchanged, and a client without the new package paused rather than guessed, for the executed fixtures; explicitly not a demonstration of general state migration or of composing arbitrary models. The binding-locality cases (V6 table) show an unrelated private attach leaving a saved intent effective, a relevant attach making the next saved intent stale_binding, and a fresh intent succeeding. The spike's negative result also applies here: authoring cost was far above budget (six semantic repairs for the Sale model), and a migration is one more thing an agent must author, so the note must budget it.\n\nThree shapes to weigh. (1) Successor scope: a new scope under the new definition whose genesis names the predecessor's final entry by fact reference and folds the predecessor's items through a declared, deterministic mapping; the predecessor enters a retired final state and forwards, refusing later acts with a reason code that names the successor. This keeps 'one head, one definition' and keeps replay as it is; its costs are a new ScopeRef that every holder must learn, new fan-in keys, re-pinned connections, and the registry-binding question R-GEN-13. (2) Redefinition entry in the same scope: a new entry kind that pins a new definition digest from the next position, with the item migration in the same entry; replay uses the definition in force at each position; stale intents are refused. This is the spike's attach. It keeps ScopeRef, items and peers; its cost is that the judge, the fold, replay, the verifier, the package checks and every statement that a scope has one definition must carry a definition per position. (3) Keep pin-for-life and make scopes small and short-lived, so a long-lived application is a lineage of scopes under one stable name that the directory resolves; the grow site already works this way for its rooms. The filer's recommendation is shape 1 for the near term, with the spike's rules applied to it unchanged (exact boundary at the predecessor's final entry, no backfill, deterministic pinned mapping, no enlargement of authority, stale acts refused), and shape 2 weighed honestly against the spike's evidence rather than dismissed. The note decides.\n\nQuestions the note must answer. Q1 the shape, and why, against the spike's rules and invariant 7. Q2 the mapping: data, not code, like the definition itself (item type to item type, state to state, slot to slot, with defaults), total and deterministic, validated by dap check, authored in the successor package that names its predecessor. Q3 who may start a succession and under what review, given C-3 and that agents hold no rules.activate: an act in the predecessor, an act in the room, or both. Q4 in-flight work at the boundary: acts signed against the predecessor after it, unanswered requests and pending sends in its dispatcher, deliveries to the old ScopeRef, deadlines held in items that timed rules watch, and retained inputs. Q5 connections: how peers whose pin names the predecessor's digest accept the successor (a declared lineage, or succession of the peers too), and what the fan-in proofs say when keys include the incarnation. Q6 views and packages: the retired scope's views need its package pinned (the history note's cohorts), the successor's views read the mapped items. Q7 naming: one stable application name across succession, the directory's part in resolving it, R-GEN-13, and the rule that a scope URI must resolve, applied to a retired scope (its record plus a forward). Q8 replay and the verifier: the successor's replay reads the predecessor's final entry by hash as deliveries do, recomputes the mapping, and the archive binding covers the predecessor's journal. Q9 retirement as a first-class state for every scope type and for packages: retire without a successor (stop intake, keep readable and replayable), and how a retire act coexists with the packages note's fan-in proof, which today refuses a site whose room type gains a retire act as fan-in-unproved. Q10 the authoring budget: what a migration costs an agent to write, measured on the first fixture.\n\nWhy before the other notes. The sales, market and matter packages will create long-lived records soon; the shape should be settled while the number of such records is small. Related requests filed today for the same OS lens: the kernel boundary page and the one-channel audit; this note should state the principle that the kernel page then carries.","body":{"conditions":"Document only: one design note under notes/ with a status line, plus the roadmap row 'Rules that change after creation' and the status convention updated when it lands. Plain English per ISO 24495-1 for a technical audience; no product names other than GitHub in the note. The note chooses one shape for definition succession and says why, weighing the three shapes in the request against the dap-spike's versioning rules 1 to 9 and its invariant 7, with exact paths into the spike's notes and reports and the executed-fixture limits the spike report states. It answers questions Q1 to Q10 of the request, names each contract and documentation statement that changes (scopes.md 'pins one definition for life', what-is-dap.md, architecture.md, the packages note's successor-package rule), and lists implementation slices in order without filing them. The first slice it lists is a fixture: the Counter or the lending definition succeeded by a new version with one added act and one renamed state, the record replayed, a stale act refused with its reason code, and the retired scope's URI resolving. Checker review before landing; land after approval.","priority":"immediate: take before the layout note and the Room fork contracts","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}